 and it's very logical  because now if black capture the knight  white will have a bishop pair and a good  center  so here  we will continue with the castling on  the queen side  and after bishop to e2 which is  unpinning the knight  black will continue with the knight to  f6  and after  castling on the king side by the white  black has a very fantastic move which is  e5  and now you can see if the knight  captured the pawn then black can simply  capture the knight and white cannot  capture the knight because this pawn is  pinned so that's why  here most logical reply by the white  will be and that has been suggested by  the fritz as well  which is to play this move h3  and now you can see the first merit is  it's attacking the bishop  now many attacking maniac will sacrifice  this bishop so for example a simple line  can goes like this so black can play  this move bishop cross h3 but after g  cross h3 queen cross h3 white has this  move which is knight to g5 and  as far as i can see it's not only  attacking the queen it's also attacking  the f7 square and the main drawback is  now this knight will never come to the  g4 square and if white play accurately  black attack will not crash through  and black will be a piece down  so instead of this move  there is a very cunning motor exists for  the black  i give you a few seconds  can you find a very amazing move over  here  okay the move is  e cross d4 and at first side it looks  like it's not possible because  not only white can capture our bishop  but now it's also attacking our queen  but here comes a very good reply by the  black which is knight to g4 now first of  all you can see this pawn is attacking  the knight  and once this pawn moves it will be a  discover attack to the queen  so here there are few replies for the  white and let's have a look each by turn  so for example  let's take the worst one first so if  white  capture this pawn then  black can capture this pawn and as far  as i can see this is almost a 