layed  like this  and i played knight to e5 so now you can  see attacking the knight  and here i think the most appropriate  move of the wide will be bishop to e2  well even in that case  black will remain  at least one pawn up and attack to boot  but in my game one of my opponent played  this move which is queen cross a7 and he  thought that  he's checkmating me in the next move  but i give you a few seconds  can you find a sequence which will  finish the white  okay the sequence start with the move  knight cross f3 and  please note if  g cross f3 then that leads to the  checkmate into queen h2 check  queen cross f2 mate  so that's not possible so  in the game my opponent played king to  f1  and he thought that he is now in very  good position but he completely  overlooked this move which is queen  check  and now the only move left for the white  is rook to e2 afterwards  can you spot it yep it's  knight to h2 checkmate  now  let's have a look it's the last move  which is  what happens if at this position white  knights will run away so for example  knight to a4 well against this we have a  very good reply start with the move  knight to e5 so now you can see it's  attacking the knight and not only that  we have a sitting target on the h2 so if  the knight moves then uh white king will  be mated at h2  and here the most logical reply by the  wide will be to first of all play rook  to e1 so giving a space to the king and  afterwards we will certainly capture  this pawn so now you can see it's not  only attacking the f2 but it also have a  discovery  to the queen  and here black play bishop cross e3 then  after rook cross queen rook cross rook  bishop to b5  attacking the rogue and after knight to  c3 we'll simply grab the knight and  after white recapture we will simply  grab the bishop and after white  recapture we can play this move which is  knight cross f3 and after bishop cross  f3 between two e5  you can see all the white pawns are  crippled  and  there is not 